---
import CompressionSpring from '@/components/diagrams/CompressionSpring.astro';
import MaterialsTable from '@/components/ui/MaterialsTable.astro';
import Todo from '@/components/ui/Todo.astro';
import { compression } from '@/data/products';
import ProductLayout from '@/layouts/ProductLayout.astro';

const ends = [
  {
    key: 'closed-ground' as const,
    label: 'Closed and ground',
    note: 'Stands flat. The usual precision end.',
  },
  {
    key: 'closed' as const,
    label: 'Closed (squared)',
    note: 'Last coil sits against the next. Not ground.',
  },
  { key: 'open' as const, label: 'Open', note: 'Plain end. Cheap, and it leans.' },
  {
    key: 'open-ground' as const,
    label: 'Open and ground',
    note: 'Ground for a flatter seat without closing the end.',
  },
];
---

<ProductLayout product={compression}>
  <Fragment slot="about">
    <p>
      A compression spring is an <strong>open-coiled helix</strong> that pushes back when you
      shorten it. It is the most common spring type, and the one whose drawing is easiest to get
      wrong: people forget solid height, end type, or the hole the spring has to live in.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three numbers decide most of the fit: <strong>wire diameter</strong>,{' '}
      <strong>outer diameter</strong> and <strong>free length</strong>. The rate follows from those
      plus the number of active coils and the material. If you also give a load at a working length,
      we can see whether the spring will bind before it does the job.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep the spring index (mean diameter ÷ wire) between about 4 and 12. Below 4 the wire is hard
      to coil; above 12 the coil wanders. A free length of four times the mean diameter or more will
      buckle unless the spring is guided on a rod or in a bore.
    </p>
    <p>
      We grind ends when the drawing asks for it. Grinding is often skipped on the finest wire in
      our range.
      <Todo text="smallest wire diameter you will grind." />
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="types">
    <ul class="grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {ends.map((e) => (
        <li class="type-fig">
          <div class="bp-grid type-draw">
            <CompressionSpring ends={e.key} decorative />
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>{e.label}.</strong> {e.note}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[46rem]">
      Cylindrical is the default. If the coils have to nest, that is a{' '}
      <a class="link" href="/springs/conical-springs/">
        conical spring
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <MaterialsTable slot="materials" />

  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <p>
      Say whether the spring <strong>works in a hole or over a rod</strong>, and give that diameter.
      An OD that looked fine on the desk can jam in a 0.2 mm-tight bore once the coil expands under
      load.
    </p>
    <p>
      Closed-and-ground ends are the right default when the spring must stand square. If the wire is
      near 0.40 mm, ask us before you dimension a grind.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="applications">
    <p>
      Switchgear and MCB contact stacks, small valves and buttons, battery contacts, furniture
      seats, instrumentation, pens and other light mechanisms. Automotive work in this wire range is
      usually a detent, a return or a seat spring — not a suspension coil.
    </p>
    <p>We do not make die springs, heavy valve springs or anything above 2.50 mm wire.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ProductLayout>

<style>
  .type-fig {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .type-draw {
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grid);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    color: var(--color-navy-900);
  }

  .type-fig p {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
